antee the quality  and you can understand that what you're  getting it also helps people change some  of their dietary choices because rather  than going through the produce and  finding some things maybe not to your  taste maybe you can get them at their  peak and maybe this is going to  influence how much of it you can eat and  have all these great plant nutrients in  your diet so this has been a novel thing  that wasn't a very fast initially to get  into marketplace luckily I have a great  ally in this process so I am very  fortunate to work with a renowned  farm-to-table chef dan barber one of the  world leaders in this and as we started  to develop this in terms of co-creation  of this new squash it was working with  him in the fields as an opportunity to  taste together see the techniques how we  get the most flavor out of it up here is  the four I was sadly to Instagram a  while ago so I started to eat it before  taking the picture where you see this  beautiful small squash something that's  convenient that fits and some family  sizes nowadays is able to be more of a  side dish rather than kind of filling up  the dishes concentrating the flavor  concentrating the nutrition and also one  of the things that does it we realized  at the time is on the plate it can  resemble what it looks like in the field  and so it's really bringing some of that  field present to you on the plate and so  that's kind of some of the farm-to-table  connection it can make and it has  received a lot of popularity it's been  described in many food magazines media  and I've seen  from seeing I was talking about a  Netflix and a great documentary also  it's achieved one of the things that is  two vegetables get to have apples will  know by variety name you might be going  to the orchards and homecoming in the  fall be getting a Snapdragon Apple so  this is a butternut squash is actually  sold by name not just a butternut squash  but honey nut squash and as we've  continued to go along there's been a lot  we can do to make the food we have  available to us we the farms they can  grow up forth more exciting more  distinctive to be able to really  showcase what we're developing give  consumers give all of us a new flavors  to try we've been in many of the seed  catalogs you'll see this year there's  the white cucumber behind me martini  that has also a fantastic yield for the  grower and a much more delicious refined  flavor than many other cucumbers you'll  have the Havana the pepper which is a  habanero pepper with the spice removed  but it keeps the flavor of a habanero  peppers you can experience all those  flavors without the burn and so it's a  great way to see all the steps of flavor  that's in the produce around us and some  diversity to really help make some of  the produce more competitive with some  of the other food choices we have and we  can continue to to push this and work  for some more ornamental edibles and try  to get peppers that are both loaded with  vitamin C and are just a nice snack size  so it's a more convenient way to go to  be eating delicious you know hopefully  compelling produce and also a plant that  even as an ornamental and it works in  many more areas and we might have  available to grow food for those of us  that might have to have more of a  balcony space than a garden space and so  in this process as I continue to be  really excited about this co-creation of  this avant-garde produce new exciting  increasing the flavor  and the appeal of that and making a more  noticeable and transcends enjoying  seeing farmers it incorporates this into  other growing I had started to really  think this is what farm-to-table was  about to be able to get all these great  new exciting things for the farmers to  grow and if I was fortunate that again  this is where chef Barbara came in to  correct me and they will no not about  that you know so we're supporting the  food system and it's also is really  about trying to figure also  